              {
                "id": "p82-q58-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Factor out ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and use the quadratic discriminant, including the triple-root case ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=x^3",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=x(x^2+px+q)\\\\\\Delta&=p^2-4q.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe origin is always an $x$-intercept. It is the only intercept when the quadratic factor has no real roots, or when both quadratic roots are zero.\n\nNo real quadratic roots requires $\\Delta<0$, giving $p^2<4q$.\n\nBoth quadratic roots are zero precisely when $p=q=0$. Then $f(x)=x^3$, with a triple root at zero.\n\nTherefore, $p^2<4q$, or $p=q=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p82-q58-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "two distinct $x$-intercepts.",
        "layout": "list",
