        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p82-q58-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "p^2>4q",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "q\\ne0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p82-q58-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The quadratic factor must have two distinct real roots, neither equal to zero.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=x(x^2+px+q)\\\\\\Delta&=p^2-4q.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe existing root is $x=0$. Two further distinct real roots require $\\Delta>0$, giving $p^2>4q$.\n\nZero is a root of the quadratic factor exactly when $q=0$. Therefore, require $q\\ne0$ so that neither quadratic root coincides with the existing root.\n\nHence, $p^2>4q$ and $q\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
